    explanationEn: "A fitting capstone to this collection, from the Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (3.2.9). It states the ultimate promise of all the Mahāvākyas: the knower of Brahman does not merely learn about Brahman as an external fact — they become Brahman. This is the unique character of spiritual knowledge in Vedānta: to know the Self is to be the Self, for here the knower, the knowing, and the known are one.\n\nThis is why the Mahāvākyas — \"Prajñānaṃ Brahma,\" \"Tat tvam asi,\" \"Ahaṃ Brahmāsmi,\" \"Ayam ātmā Brahma\" — are not mere doctrines to be believed but truths to be realized. They function as instruments of transformation. To fully understand them is, in the same act, to be liberated. Knowledge here is not information stored; it is identity transfigured.\n\n🌐 Modern Resonance: This dissolves the modern divide between knowing and being. Most knowledge leaves the knower unchanged. But the deepest self-knowledge transforms the very one who knows. The journey of all fifty utterances ends here: the seeker, having known the Real, is no longer separate from it.",
